<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="734" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/what-we-handle/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Appraisal Clause Representation</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Appraisal Clause Representation for Kansas Policyholders</h1><p class="lede">Once a written appraisal demand is on the table, the clock and the stakes both change. We become the appraiser you name, rebuild the valuation from the ground up and carry it through the panel.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line" href="/request-kansas-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Your role</dt><dd>You appoint us</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Binds</dt><dd>Amount of loss</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>The short paragraph that changes a claim</h2>
<p>Most Kansas homeowners, farm and commercial property forms include a condition titled “Appraisal.” In plain terms, it says that if you and the insurer can’t agree on the amount of loss, either side can demand appraisal in writing. Each side picks a competent and impartial appraiser, those two pick an umpire, and a written agreement signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p>
<p>That structure turns an open-ended disagreement into a closed process with a finish line. It also means the person you appoint carries real weight: the umpire will weigh their documentation against the carrier’s.</p>
<h2>What we handle as your appraiser</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Policy and deadline check.</strong> Some forms give 20 days to name an appraiser after a demand; others are silent. We read your form before anything else.</li>
<li><strong>Independent inspection.</strong> We measure, photograph and test the property ourselves, from the ridge to the basement floor drain.</li>
<li><strong>Line-item valuation.</strong> We build an estimate priced for your part of Kansas, whether that is Johnson County labor rates or a western Kansas job where crews travel two hours.</li>
<li><strong>Working the panel.</strong> We meet the insurer’s appraiser, trade estimates and narrow the dispute to the items that genuinely differ.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire presentation.</strong> If items stay open, we present them to the umpire with photos, measurements and pricing support.</li>
<li><strong>Award review.</strong> We make sure the award form states replacement cost, actual cash value and any holdback clearly.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">A careful word on Kansas law</b>Appraisal decides the amount of loss, not coverage. Kansas has statutes under which a court may award attorney fees to a policyholder in some insurance lawsuits (K.S.A. 40-256, and K.S.A. 40-908 for policies covering fire, tornado, lightning or hail). Whether either applies depends on facts we can’t judge. We are not lawyers; ask a Kansas attorney about legal questions.</div>
<h2>When representation fits, and when it doesn’t</h2>
<p>Appraisal works best when the insurer has accepted at least part of the claim and the fight is about scope or price: how many slopes are hail-damaged, whether siding can be matched, what a tear-off actually costs in Sedgwick County after a big storm. It is a poor fit for outright denials, “wear and tear” arguments that are really coverage questions, or claims close to a suit deadline.</p>
<h2>How we keep numbers credible</h2>
<p>We would rather tell you an honest range on day one than hand you a number that collapses in front of the umpire. Credibility with the panel is what moves awards, so every line we write is one we can support.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary><span>Can my roofer serve as my appraiser?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>It is usually a bad idea. Most appraisal clauses require an impartial or disinterested appraiser, and a contractor who stands to win the repair job invites a challenge to the award.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Does the insurer have to take part once I demand appraisal?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>When the policy has an appraisal clause and the dispute is over the amount of loss, the carrier generally must participate after a proper written demand. Coverage disputes may be carved out.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>How long does a Kansas appraisal usually take?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>A straightforward residential roof or siding dispute often wraps up in six to ten weeks after both appraisers are named. Commercial losses and umpire hearings take longer.</p></div></details></div>
